===Etymology 1===
{{ja-kanjitab|かい|y=k}}
Attested phonographically in [[w:Old Japanese|Old Japanese]] with the pronunciation ''kai''. This word is unique in having adjacent vowels word-medially, a feature generally disallowed in Old Japanese. There are a few theories to explain this, with the most accepted theory being that this is the {{ja-r|イ音%便|い-おん%びん|i-[[euphony]]}} form of {{ja-r|掻き|かき|pos=the {{ja-r|連%用%形|れん%よう%けい|conjunctive form}} of the verb {{ja-r|掻く|かく|to [[scratch]]}}}}. Issues with the chronology of that theory (particularly {{ja-r|音%便|おん%びん|euphony|lit=sound convenience}} only being a common process in later forms of Japanese) have led to other theories, including:
* Bound form ''*ka-'' + ''-i'', a suffix for making standalone forms (compare {{m|ja|酒|tr=sake < sake<sub>2</sub> < *sakai}}), but the lack of vowel fusion is unexplained.
* {{ja-r|連%用%形|れん%よう%けい}} of a hypothetical {{ja-r|上%二%段%活%用|かみ% に%だん% かつ%よう|upper bigrade conjugation}} verb ''*kayu'', i.e. ''*kayi > kai''.<ref>{{R:ja:NKD Concise}}</ref>

